      "notes": "CONSOLIDATION: 21 brainstorm entries collapse to 18 distinct candidates. The three EM entries (canonical persona, attention-synergist, in-context-trigger) all lose for one reason — EM is an MLP/residual OUTPUT direction (five independent lines incl. this repo's em-svd-steer double-dissociation) — so I merged them into one rank-12 entry. The two retrieval entries (real Wu NIAH vs scoped single-needle) are kept separate because they have OPPOSITE verdicts: the real benchmark is infeasible+redundant (rank 14, lose), the scoped bridge is feasible+already-won (rank 3, win) — this split IS the scientifically interesting boundary. The two backtracking entries are kept separate: ONSET is direction-routed (rank 16, lose, owned by Venhoff/Ward), LOCALIZATION is the only opening (rank 5, uncertain).\n\nVALIDATED GROUND TRUTH from the repo (summary.md, FRA_PRINCIPLE.md, CAMPAIGN_LOG.md): the CCF∧LBNR predictor is already validated on every prior case. Existing WINS: induction 15x, copy-suppression L10H7 22.7x (a NEW result the screen predicted in advance — CCF flagged, LBNR confirmed, Tier-2 delivered), retrieval-gemma 16x, in-context backdoor 25x. Existing LOSSES with measured R: IOI R=-0.39 (backups), binding R=-0.89, greater-than R=0.11 (MLP-routed). The reusable g_screen.py (jobs/g_screen.py) takes a JSON CANDS env var and runs CCF+LBNR for ANY probe on gpt2 OR gemma — so the entire shortlist is testable with NEAR-ZERO new code (just fill CANDS). This is the single most important feasibility fact: the gates are cheap and the infra exists.\n\nWHAT IS NOT WORTH GPU: ranks 9-18. All are either (a) direction-routed (EM, FV, refusal/harmfulness, backtracking-onset, weight-baked sleeper, MSJ — DoM/SVD already surgical, no bilinear gap), (b) MLP-downstream (successor-head increment, greater-than — the behavior is computed past the edge, the QK edge only reads the input), (c) positional (binding — order-code, CCF zeroes it; the repo already measured R=-0.89), or (d) distributed/redundant AND infeasible (real NIAH 12-50 heads, thought-anchors 512 heads, both >=6B no-SAE models). Refusal/harmfulness on gemma-2-2b-it is the ONLY one of these worth even a single cheap LBNR cut — to bank a clean negative that sharpens the direction-vs-edge taxonomy (it triple-fails: direction-routed + Prakash's dormant backup features + MLP-downstream decision). Do not run full campaigns on any of these.\n\nKEY SKEPTICAL CAVEATS on the shortlist: (1) Docstring is mechanistically a naturalistic cousin of induction — its value is external validity (a standard third-party benchmark), not a new mechanism class; the risk is gemma spreading retrieval across many induction heads (partial R). (2) Acronym's risk is CCF, not LBNR (its LBNR is published-strong, the inverse of most failures) — if word-selection is positional/BOS-mediated the bilinear edit can't express it and a position-patch ties; the CCF probe is decisive and cheap. (3) Fact-recall has the largest payoff but the worst LBNR risk profile (150-head distribution + 31% MLP-fallback + documented Hydra self-repair on this exact CounterFact task) — gate hard, expect partial R, treat R>0.8 as a surprise. (4) Reflection-localization has an unverified feasibility floor (does gemma-2-2b reflect at all?) AND a recompute-bypass risk (model re-derives from the question, never attends to the error) — the metric MUST be error-quoting not final-answer-accuracy, or it measures the wrong thing. (5) The retrieval bridge is the safest win but the least novel (largely redundant with r1/r2). PRIORITY ORDER reflects expected-NEW-value x feasibility: acronym and docstring are the cheapest decisive gates; fact-recall is the highest-upside gamble; reflection is the most fragile."
